// batch ag: Dunkirk, Battle of Britain, Pearl Harbor, Moscow, Leningrad, Tobruk
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "blitzkrieg": "'Lightning war', the name given to the German style of 1939 to 1941: tanks and aircraft punch through one point of the enemy line, push deep and cut its connections, instead of fighting head-on.",
  "panzer": "Tank, in German (from Panzerkampfwagen, 'armored combat vehicle'). A panzer division brought together tanks, motorized infantry and artillery.",
  "wehrmacht": "The armed forces of Nazi Germany: army, navy and air force.",
  "luftwaffe": "The German air force of the Second World War.",
  "radar": "A device that sends out radio waves and picks up the echo bouncing back from aircraft. It shows where a plane is dozens of kilometers away, even at night or through clouds.",
  "porta-aviões": "A ship with a long deck on top, from which aircraft take off and land. It lets a navy strike a target hundreds of kilometers away without needing an airfield nearby.",
  "torpedo": "A self-propelled underwater missile, launched from ships, submarines or aircraft. It explodes on hitting the hull, below the waterline.",
  "bombardeiro de mergulho": "An aircraft that plunges almost vertically onto its target before releasing the bomb, which makes for great accuracy. The German Stuka and the Japanese Val were of this type.",
  "perímetro": "The line that runs around the defended area. At Dunkirk and Tobruk, it was the ring of defenses around the port.",
  "rasputitsa": "A Russian word for the season of mud, in spring and autumn, when rain and thaw turn dirt roads into bogs.",
  "eixo": "The alliance of Germany, Italy and Japan in the Second World War, plus a few smaller countries.",
  "aliados": "In the case of the Second World War, the countries at war against the Axis: the United Kingdom, France, the Soviet Union, the United States and many others.",
  "caça": "A fast, agile aircraft built to fight other aircraft. The British Spitfire and Hurricane and the German Bf 109 were fighters.",
  "bombardeiro": "A large, slow, heavy aircraft built to carry bombs to the target. It needed escort fighters.",
  "contratorpedeiro": "A small, fast warship, used to escort other ships, hunt submarines and attack with torpedoes.",
  "ração": "The amount of food each person receives per day in a situation of scarcity, measured out and handed out by an authority.",
  "canhão antitanque": "A long-barreled gun, small and low, built to pierce the armor of tanks. Hidden in the ground, it is hard to see.",
  "tanque": "An armored combat vehicle on tracks, with a gun on top.",
  "silêncio de rádio": "A rule of using no radio at all so that the enemy cannot listen in and find out where the fleet is.",
  "evacuação": "The organized removal of people from a dangerous place, in this case of encircled soldiers."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  dunquerque: {
    war: "The Battle of France, in the Second World War (May and June 1940)",
    hook: "A defeated army, surrounded and backed against the sea, got home in nine days, aboard warships and pleasure boats.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In September 1939, Germany invaded Poland, and the United Kingdom and France declared war. For months, on the western front, almost nothing happened. The United Kingdom sent the British Expeditionary Force to France, with ten divisions in three corps, under Lord Gort.",
        "The original German plan to attack France resembled that of 1914. But General Erich von Manstein proposed something else: send the tanks through the Ardennes, a region of forests that the French thought too difficult for armor, and race to the English Channel. Hitler approved a version of this plan on 17 February 1940. It later became known as Sichelschnitt, the 'sickle cut'.",
        { h: "The road to the beach" },
        { timeline: [
          ["10 May", "Germany attacks the Netherlands and Belgium. The best Allied armies advance into Belgium. Three [[panzer|panzer]] corps of Army Group A move through the Ardennes."],
          ["20 May", "German tanks reach the Channel and split the Allied armies in two."],
          ["21 May", "An Allied counterattack near Arras fails to cut off the German spearhead."],
          ["23 and 24 May", "The Germans halt the advance on Dunkirk. Calais, under siege, falls on 26 May."],
          ["26 May", "Churchill orders Operation Dynamo, the withdrawal by sea."],
          ["28 May", "The Belgian army surrenders."],
          ["4 June", "The Germans enter Dunkirk. The evacuation ends."]
        ]},
        "On 19 May, Gort met the French general Billotte and found out that there were no French troops between the Germans and the sea. Around the same time, in the United Kingdom, Admiral Bertram Ramsay began planning the withdrawal, from Dover. The French were not told. By 21 May, the Allied armies in the north were trapped on the coast: the British Force, what was left of Belgium and three French armies."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Lord Gort, commander of the British Expeditionary Force" },
        "A decorated soldier of the First World War, Gort quickly saw that the situation was lost. General Weygand, French supreme commander since 18 May, wanted a counterattack to the south. Gort preferred to save the army: he sent his troops to the coast as early as 25 May and prepared the withdrawal before he had orders to do so. The decision was made without consulting the French and left lasting resentment.",
        { h: "Bertram Ramsay, the admiral of Dover" },
        "He ran the naval operation from a room dug into the cellars of Dover Castle, next to a dynamo room. According to the most often told version, the operation's name, Dynamo, comes from that room, but English Heritage says there is no proof of this. Ramsay was given very little time and very few ships for a task nobody had done before.",
        { h: "Hitler and Rundstedt" },
        "General Gerd von Rundstedt commanded Army Group A. Worried about his flanks, his supply line, the canal-soaked terrain and his tank losses, he approved a halt order on the night of 23 May. Hitler confirmed it on 24 May and left it to Göring's [[luftwaffe|luftwaffe]] to finish off the pocket. The order was cancelled on the afternoon of 26 May.",
        { h: "Churchill" },
        "He had been prime minister for two weeks. On 26 May he ordered Operation Dynamo and, on 4 June, gave a speech in Parliament that mixed two feelings: he called the episode a 'colossal military disaster', but also a 'miracle of deliverance', and warned that 'wars are not won by evacuations'."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the German side, about 800,000 men in the region, with tanks that were tired and had suffered many losses. On the Allied side, about 400,000 men: the British Force, three French armies and what remained of the Belgian army. The numbers vary a little depending on the source.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Dunkirk is a port on the north coast of France, next to the Belgian border. The ground around it is flat, cut by canals and floodable areas, bad for tanks. The beaches are wide and shallow: large ships cannot come near, and the soldiers had to wait on the sand and in water up to their waists or shoulders.",
        "The defended [[perimeter|perímetro]] was a semicircle, with the French in the west and the British in the east. It ran from the Belgian coast, near Nieuwpoort, to Gravelines, passing through Bergues. It shrank bit by bit until the end of May.",
        { h: "The tank pause" },
        "When the tanks stopped, the port was within their reach. The halt gave the Allies time to organize the defense and the withdrawal. Why it happened is still debated. Rundstedt and General Kluge proposed gathering forces and leaving the destruction of the pocket to the Luftwaffe. Göring promised that his aircraft would do the job. According to the historian Liddell Hart, Hitler thought the British would never return to Europe.",
        { note: "Some said that Hitler let the British escape on purpose. Most historians doubt this thesis: the German army's own chiefs regretted the pause afterward. Guderian, a tank commander, later wrote that not attacking Dunkirk was a great mistake. The length of the pause also changes depending on the source: from the night of 23 May until the afternoon of 26 May, that is, between two and three days." },
        { h: "The withdrawal from the beach" },
        "The port of Dunkirk was destroyed by bombing right at the start. So the naval officer William Tennant, who directed the embarkation, used the two moles, long structures of concrete and wood that stretch out to sea, built to dock barges and not warships. About 200,000 men embarked from the east mole. On the beaches of De Panne and Bray-Dunes, soldiers even built improvised piers out of abandoned trucks.",
        "What helped a great deal were the small boats: yachts, river barges, fishing boats, lifeboats, pleasure launches. Some were requisitioned, and many crossed with civilian crews. They began arriving on 28 May, crossed the Channel and carried the soldiers from the beach to the larger ships, which lay farther out.",
        { timeline: [
          ["27 May", "7,669 men embark. About 1,000 civilians die in the bombing of the town."],
          ["28 May", "17,804 embark."],
          ["29 May", "47,310 embark. After this day, the Navy withdraws its eight best destroyers for home defense."],
          ["30 May", "53,823 embark."],
          ["31 May", "The peak day: 68,014 embark. At Lille, the French surrender, after holding seven German divisions for four days."],
          ["1 June", "64,429 embark."],
          ["2 to 4 June", "About 26,000 per day. The British leave on 3 June. The following night, the Navy returns to fetch the French rear guard."]
        ]},
        "The daily numbers add up to 338,226 men, 98,671 of them from the beaches and 239,555 from the moles and the harbor. More than 100,000 were French. According to one count, 861 vessels were used, of which 243 sank. The Royal Navy lost six destroyers and the French Navy lost three.",
        { h: "The sky" },
        "Many soldiers blamed the Royal Air Force for not seeing it over the beaches. In fact, most of the air fighting took place far off, over the interior, and the soldiers below could not see it. The RAF lost 145 aircraft, at least 42 of them Spitfires. The Luftwaffe lost 156, including 35 shot down by Navy ships.",
        { h: "Lille and the rear guard" },
        "Between 28 and 31 May, the French First Army, surrounded near Lille, held seven German divisions, three of them armored. According to one estimate, this helped save about 100,000 Allied soldiers. The Germans granted the defenders the honors of war. The surrender at Lille, on 31 May, yielded between 35,000 and 40,000 prisoners, depending on the source. On 4 June, after the last embarkations, about 35,000 soldiers who were still on the beach, most of them French from the 2nd Light Mechanized Division and the 68th Infantry, were also captured."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Germany took the port and captured most of the equipment. The British Expeditionary Force left behind 2,472 guns, about 20,000 motorcycles and nearly 65,000 vehicles, and almost all of the roughly 445 tanks it had taken to France. For every seven men who escaped, one was left behind as a prisoner of war, and many were marched to Germany. Between 10 May and 22 June, the British army had about 68,000 casualties.",
        "But the 338,226 men who came back were the core of an army that could still fight. On 22 June France signed the armistice. Another operation, Aerial, took more than 190,000 Allied soldiers out of other French ports between 15 and 25 June.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Dunkirk was a military defeat that became a symbol of resistance. For the United Kingdom, it remained as 'the Dunkirk spirit': the idea that ordinary people rally when the situation is desperate. This 'people's war' picture, spread by books and films, is what stayed in popular memory. Historians point out that other battles were going on at the same time, at Calais, Boulogne and Lille, and that the troops in those places bought the time the withdrawal used.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The French felt abandoned. The decision to evacuate rather than counterattack to the south was the British one, made without consulting them. Afterward, Admiral Darlan ordered that priority be given to the British, and only on 31 May did he order the French to embark on equal terms." },
        { note: "Casualty counts vary. One of the most used gives the Allied side about 61,774 killed, wounded and captured, but the same page gives other figures for the French (18,000 dead and 40,000 prisoners) and the British (68,111 casualties, including about 41,000 prisoners). They measure different periods and different things and should not be added together carelessly." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  inglaterra: {
    war: "Second World War: the air campaign over Britain (July to October 1940)",
    hook: "For the first time, a major military campaign was decided in the air alone, and the winner was the side that could see the enemy coming.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In June 1940, France fell, and the United Kingdom stood alone against Germany. Hitler hoped London would negotiate peace. Churchill refused. On 18 June, he said in Parliament: 'I expect that the Battle of Britain is about to begin'. The battle's name, therefore, predates it.",
        "On 19 July, Hitler gave a speech offering talks, and the British government rejected it. Three days earlier, on 16 July, he had signed Directive No. 16, which ordered preparations for the invasion of the United Kingdom, Operation Sea Lion, for mid-August. The German navy warned that crossing the Channel without control of the air was too risky. So everything depended on the [[luftwaffe|luftwaffe]] defeating the Royal Air Force (RAF).",
        { h: "The road to the August sky" },
        { timeline: [
          ["10 July", "By the official British date, the battle begins. In the first weeks, the Germans attack convoys and ports in the Channel."],
          ["1 August", "Directive No. 17 orders the Luftwaffe to destroy the RAF."],
          ["13 August", "Adlertag, 'Eagle Day': the great attack begins, delayed by bad weather."],
          ["24 August to 6 September", "The period of greatest pressure on Fighter Command's airfields."],
          ["7 September", "The Germans begin attacking London. The Blitz begins, lasting until 11 May 1941."],
          ["15 September", "The biggest day of fighting. Two days later, Hitler postpones the invasion."],
          ["31 October", "Official end of the battle."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hugh Dowding, head of Fighter Command" },
        "Dowding had led Fighter Command since the 1936 reorganization. He rejected the idea, widely accepted at the time, that 'the bomber will always get through', and bet on fighters and radar. When the French asked for more squadrons in May 1940, he opposed sending them, because it would weaken home defense. His central worry was not a lack of aircraft, but of trained pilots.",
        { h: "Keith Park and Trafford Leigh-Mallory" },
        "Park commanded 11 Group, in southeast England, which bore the brunt of the attacks. Leigh-Mallory commanded 12 Group, farther north, which covered 11 Group's airfields. The two disagreed over tactics: Leigh-Mallory advocated the 'big wings', groups of several squadrons attacking together; Park preferred to send out individual squadrons, quicker to get airborne. Sources differ on the value of the big wing, and the quarrel is tied to Dowding's replacement in November 1940.",
        { h: "Hermann Göring and the Luftwaffe" },
        "Head of the Luftwaffe and a central figure of the regime, Göring divided the campaign among three air fleets, with chiefs who disagreed over targets. Sperrle preferred to attack the British air defense structure, and Kesselring to attack London. German intelligence got a great deal wrong: it overestimated British losses and underestimated the strength of the RAF's fighters."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The RAF had about 1,963 aircraft, and the Luftwaffe about 2,550, by the most cited counts. In the first months, the Hurricane was the RAF's most numerous fighter, at a ratio of about two to one against the Spitfire. The German Bf 109E was up to 65 km/h faster than a Hurricane with certain propellers, depending on altitude, and had two 20 mm cannons and two machine guns. The British had eight .303 caliber machine guns. The Bf 110, a twin-engine fighter, was a poor escort and suffered heavily. The Stuka dive bombers took such high losses on 18 August that they almost ceased to be used.",
        "The RAF's problem was pilots. On 1 July the RAF could muster about 1,103 fighter pilots. In total, 2,936 pilots flew at least one operational sortie during the battle, and 595 of them were not British, including 145 Poles and 88 Czechoslovaks. No. 303 Squadron, Polish, scored the most victories, despite having entered the fight about two months after the start.",
        { h: "The Dowding system" },
        "What most set the two sides apart was information. [[radar|radar]] stations on the coast, the Chain Home, and the Observer Corps, volunteers who tracked aircraft by eye and by telephone, sent what they saw to headquarters at Bentley Priory. There, a filter room merged the reports into a single map, and the data went down to the groups and to the sectors, from which controllers sent fighters up by radio. The RAF only took off where and when it needed to.",
        "Radar saw only what lay in front of the antennas, generally over the sea, and the observers covered the land. Before the war, an interception rate of 30% to 50% was considered excellent. During the battle, interceptions succeeded more than 75% of the time, according to one estimate, and about 90%, according to another.",
        { h: "August: the airfields" },
        "After weeks of attacks on ships in the Channel, Adlertag, on 13 August, opened the central phase. The Luftwaffe began attacking airfields, radar stations and aircraft factories. On the worst days, Bf 109 fighters were ordered to protect 300 to 400 bombers. But the 109's range was limited, and over London there was little fuel left to fight.",
        { h: "Late August and early September" },
        "Between 24 August and 6 September, the pressure on the southeastern airfields was enormous. Historians disagree about the real danger. Wood and Dempster count, for this period, 295 fighters destroyed and 103 pilots killed or missing, and think the RAF ran a serious risk. Bungay, Dye and Overy state that the RAF never ran out of pilots and that the attacks on airfields caused little lasting damage.",
        { h: "7 September: the target changes" },
        "According to many historians, after British bombers attacked Berlin in late August, Hitler began to give more attention to London and to night bombing. The first big daylight attack on the capital was on 7 September and killed or wounded about 1,600 civilians. The military effect was the opposite of what the Germans wanted: 11 Group's airfields got a breather, and the RAF was able to rebuild.",
        { h: "15 September" },
        "The Germans planned a big daylight offensive over London to force the RAF into a decisive battle. Around 475 German aircraft took part in the afternoon raid, and the RAF had about 276 fighters in the air at the same time at its peak. Churchill, in the operations room of 11 Group at Uxbridge, asked Park whether there were still reserves. Park answered that there were none. Cloud scattered many German formations, and the raid did less damage than intended.",
        "British pilots claimed 81 aircraft shot down in the midday phase alone, and the RAF in total said it had downed 77 bombers and 29 fighters. Germany's real losses were far smaller: about 21 to 23 bombers and at least 12 fighters. It was an exaggeration, common on both sides, and did not change the result. On 17 September, Hitler postponed Sea Lion."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The RAF lost about 1,744 aircraft and the Luftwaffe about 1,977, by the most cited counts. Death tolls vary depending on what is included: 1,542 British and 2,585 Germans is one of the most cited, and another 925 Germans were captured. The Luftwaffe switched to night bombing, the Blitz, which lasted until May 1941. The invasion never happened. British bombers had attacked the invasion barges in the Channel ports and sunk about 200 by 19 September.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first major military campaign fought entirely by air forces. It showed that defense, with radar and communications, could beat a larger attacker. It prevented the invasion and kept the United Kingdom as the base from which the Allies would later attack Europe. On 20 August, Churchill said in Parliament, of the pilots, that never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Churchill's 'few' came from many countries. Of the 2,936 recognized pilots, 595 were not British." },
        { note: "Numbers of pilots and aircraft vary by source and by period. The figures above are the most cited. The figure for civilians killed also depends on where the count is cut off: the Blitz continued after the official end of the battle." },
        { note: "Dowding was replaced in November 1940, by Sholto Douglas, after the victory. His departure is associated with the dispute with Leigh-Mallory over the big wings." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  pearlharbor: {
    war: "Start of the war in the Pacific (December 1941)",
    hook: "On a Sunday morning, six aircraft carriers that nobody saw coming tried to wipe out the American fleet in two hours, and woke the country that decided the war.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Since 1937, Japan had been at war in China. The country, poor in oil, depended on importing it. In 1941, after Japan occupied French Indochina, the United States cut off oil exports to it. Japan turned its eyes to the oil of the Dutch East Indies, in Southeast Asia, and feared that the American fleet, in Hawaii, would intervene.",
        "Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto, head of the Combined Fleet, began studying an idea in early 1941: destroy the American battleships all at once, before they could react. The planners studied the British attack on the Italian fleet at Taranto, in 1940, carried out with aircraft launched from carriers.",
        { h: "The road to Pearl Harbor" },
        { timeline: [
          ["July 1941", "The United States cuts oil exports to Japan."],
          ["5 November", "Emperor Hirohito approves the plan. Final authorization comes on 1 December."],
          ["20 November", "Japan makes a last proposal: leave southern Indochina in exchange for aviation fuel and an end to the sanctions."],
          ["26 November", "The United States answers with the Hull note, which demands that Japan leave China. On the same day, the Japanese fleet sails from the Kurils, before the note arrives."],
          ["7 December", "Sunday. The attack."]
        ]},
        "Diplomacy went on while the fleet was at sea."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Isoroku Yamamoto, the mastermind" },
        "An admiral, he had lived in the United States and knew its industrial power. He wanted a blow so strong, right at the start, that it would buy Japan time in the Pacific. A line attributed to a Japanese officer, Hara, sums up the outcome bitterly: Japan won a great tactical victory at Pearl Harbor and, by doing so, lost the war. The exact origin of the line is not clear.",
        { h: "Chūichi Nagumo, commander of the strike fleet" },
        "Nagumo commanded the task force with the six carriers. It was his decision, after the attack, not to send a third wave.",
        { h: "Minoru Genda and Mitsuo Fuchida" },
        "Genda was the air planner who helped put the operation together. Fuchida led the first wave of aircraft. Shigekazu Shimazaki commanded the second.",
        { h: "Husband Kimmel and Walter Short" },
        "Kimmel commanded the American Pacific fleet, and Short, the army in Hawaii. Both were later criticized for not having prepared for an air attack and were removed from their posts."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Japanese force, the Kido Butai, had six aircraft carriers, two battleships, three cruisers, eleven destroyers and eight oilers. It carried, in total, 353 aircraft that took part in the two waves. The first wave had about 180 aircraft, the second about 170. The planners meant to leave 48 fighters as air patrol over the fleet.",
        "In the harbor were eight American battleships, several cruisers and destroyers. The three aircraft carriers of the Pacific fleet, Enterprise, Lexington and Saratoga, were away from Pearl Harbor that day.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "On 26 November 1941, the fleet sailed from Hitokappu Bay, on Etorofu island, in the Kurils. It went under [[radio silence|silêncio de rádio]] along a northern route, to reach a point north of Hawaii. The idea was to launch the aircraft from there, in two waves.",
        { h: "Before dawn" },
        { formation: true },
        "Five larger submarines carried midget submarines to near Oahu, and launched them around 1 a.m. At 3:42 a.m., the minesweeper Condor spotted a periscope and warned the destroyer Ward. The Ward sank a midget submarine at 6:37 a.m. These were the first American shots of the Pacific war. A second midget submarine would be sunk by the Monaghan at 8:43 a.m.",
        { h: "The warning that was not taken seriously" },
        "The first wave was detected by the Army's [[radar|radar]] at Opana Point, from more than 200 km away (the sources cite between 130 and 140 miles). The set was in training, and two soldiers, Elliott and Lockard, warned the information center. Lieutenant Kermit Tyler thought it was six B-17 bombers due in from California. The operators found the signal much larger than normal, but Tyler told them not to worry.",
        { h: "The attack" },
        "Fuchida's wave attacked at 7:48 a.m., Hawaii time. The [[torpedo|torpedo]] aircraft went straight for the battleships, moored in pairs beside Ford Island. The level bombers dropped armor-piercing bombs, and the [[dive bombers|bombardeiro de mergulho]] and [[fighters|caça]] attacked the airfields, to destroy the American aircraft on the ground.",
        "The Arizona was hit by four armor-piercing bombs and exploded, with a forward magazine. 1,177 men died, nearly half of all American deaths. The Oklahoma capsized, with 429 dead. The Nevada ran aground, and the West Virginia sank.",
        "The second wave, with about 170 aircraft, came roughly an hour later and attacked mainly airfields and ships that were still afloat. In all, the action lasted about 90 minutes.",
        { h: "The third wave that never left" },
        "Nagumo recovered his aircraft and left without sending a third wave. Among the reasons: American antiaircraft fire grew stronger, nobody knew where the American carriers were, there was the risk of land-based bombers, landing on carriers at night was difficult, fuel was limited, and he considered the main objective achieved. The captains of the other five carriers said they were ready for another strike. Yamamoto backed Nagumo, but said afterward that not having ordered the third wave was a great mistake.",
        "The shipyard, the workshops and the big fuel tanks were left intact. For many historians, this mattered more than the battleships. Admiral Nimitz said that if they had been destroyed, the war would have lasted two more years."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "2,403 Americans died: 2,008 sailors, 109 marines, 218 soldiers and 68 civilians. 1,178 were wounded. Of the eight battleships, four sank and all suffered damage. Six returned to service, among them the Nevada, in October 1942, and the West Virginia, in July 1944. The Arizona and the Oklahoma did not return. More than 180 American aircraft were destroyed, 188 by the most cited count.",
        "Japan lost 29 aircraft, had 74 damaged and about 129 dead. A midget submarine pilot, Kazuo Sakamaki, became the first Japanese prisoner of war of the United States.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "On 8 December, President Roosevelt told Congress that 7 December was 'a date which will live in infamy'. Congress voted the declaration of war in about an hour. On 11 December, Germany and Italy declared war on the United States, and the war in the Pacific became part of a single world war.",
        "The tactics were brilliant, but the strategy failed. The United States, which had been far from entering the war, entered united. The carriers that escaped would be a central piece of the war in the Pacific.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Japan's declaration of war was supposed to be delivered before the attack. The message, in 14 parts, was decrypted by the Americans beforehand, but communication delays prevented the warning. It was delivered more than an hour after the attack began. The historian Takeo Iguchi showed in 1999 that the Japanese army and navy resisted giving warning in advance, which refutes the old idea that it was just an accident." },
        { note: "Conspiracy theories say that the Americans and British knew of the attack and let it happen. Historians in general reject them, for lack of evidence." },
        { note: "The count of aircraft and of midget submarines varies by source. There are counts of 353 and of 414 aircraft, and of four or five midget submarines lost. We use the most cited values." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  moscou: {
    war: "Eastern Front of the Second World War: Operation Typhoon (1941 to 1942)",
    hook: "Thirty kilometers from the Kremlin, the army that had conquered Europe stopped. The mud, the cold and the divisions from Siberia did the rest.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "On 22 June 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union in Operation Barbarossa. Army Group Center advanced toward Moscow and took Smolensk on 16 July. Then Hitler made a choice that the generals criticized: he sent part of the forces north, toward Leningrad, and south, toward Ukraine. The army chiefs, such as Brauchitsch and Halder, wanted a direct blow at Moscow. Brauchitsch was told that only 'ossified brains' could think otherwise.",
        "The detour paid off at Kiev: the Wehrmacht closed a huge pocket in September, and the Red Army, there alone, lost close to 700,000 men killed, wounded and captured, by 26 September. After that, with summer ending, Hitler turned back to Moscow. Operation Typhoon restarted the advance on 30 September.",
        { h: "The road to Moscow" },
        { timeline: [
          ["22 June", "The invasion of the Soviet Union begins."],
          ["16 July", "The Germans take Smolensk."],
          ["26 September", "The battle of Kiev ends, with enormous Soviet losses."],
          ["30 September", "Operation Typhoon begins. The official date of the battle is 2 October."],
          ["10 October", "The tanks meet at Vyazma. Zhukov takes over the defense of Moscow."],
          ["7 November", "Parade in Red Square, with the troops going straight to the front."],
          ["5 December", "The Soviet counteroffensive begins."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Fedor von Bock, Army Group Center" },
        "An experienced field marshal, Bock commanded three infantry armies, three [[panzer|panzer]] groups and the air fleet Luftflotte 2. Among his subordinates were Guderian, with the 2nd Panzer Group, and Hoepner, with the 4th. The plan was to encircle the Soviet forces in front of Moscow, in pockets, and only then advance on the city. Bock ended up removed, 'for health reasons', after the failure.",
        { h: "Georgy Zhukov, defender of Moscow" },
        "Zhukov took over the defense on 10 October, with Konev as his deputy. He concentrated forces at four points and later disagreed with Stalin about counterattacks launched too early, for lack of reserves. When the time came, he commanded the counteroffensive.",
        { h: "Stalin" },
        "On 15 October, Stalin ordered the government and the party evacuated to Kuibyshev. There was panic in the city. He, however, stayed in Moscow, and the news that he was there carried enormous weight. On 7 November, he ordered the usual parade held in Red Square."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "On the German side, up to two million men, between 1,000 and 2,470 tanks and assault guns and about 14,000 guns were used, by the most cited counts, with Luftflotte 2 down to only 549 aircraft fit to fly. On the Soviet side, three fronts (Western, Reserve and Bryansk), with about 1.25 million men, 1,000 tanks and 7,600 guns. The numbers vary depending on the source and on how the force is delimited.",
        { h: "Vyazma and Bryansk" },
        "In early October, the panzer groups closed two pockets, at Vyazma and at Bryansk, and their points of attack met at Vyazma on 10 October. Four Soviet armies were surrounded. Estimates of prisoners differ: German assessments speak of 673,000, recent research of about 514,000. The Germans needed 28 divisions to eliminate the pockets.",
        { h: "The mud" },
        "Around 7 October, the first snow melted and the roads turned to mud. This is the [[rasputitsa|rasputitsa]]. German tanks and trucks slowed down and the Red Army gained time to reorganize. The Mozhaisk defense line, improvised, had at first only about 90,000 soldiers. The Germans took Mozhaisk on 18 October and Volokolamsk on 27 October. At Tula, to the south, Guderian's attack reached the outskirts on 26 October, but the defense held on 29 October, and the city was never taken. On 31 October, the German command halted the offensives until supply improved.",
        { note: "Moscow's diggers were, in large part, women and teenagers. They moved almost three million cubic meters of earth to build the city's defenses." },
        { h: "The last advance" },
        "The ground froze around 15 November, and the panzers could advance again, toward Klin, Solnechnogorsk and Istra. On 27 November, the Germans had a bridgehead over the Moscow-Volga canal, less than 35 km from the Kremlin. Krasnaya Polyana was about 29 km from the Kremlin. On 2 December, a reconnaissance battalion reached Khimki, about 30 km away, the closest point the Germans reached.",
        "Winter arrived without winter clothing for the Germans. Bock recorded −45 °C on 30 November, but the official Soviet records show a low of −28.8 °C in December. More than 130,000 cases of frostbite were reported among the Germans. The Soviets, better equipped, sometimes made use of the clothes of dead Germans. On 5 December, Guderian halted his troops and wrote that the offensive had failed and that the enemy had been underestimated.",
        { h: "The counteroffensive" },
        "Reinforcements came from Siberia and the Far East: about 18 divisions, 1,700 tanks and more than 1,500 aircraft, after the spy Richard Sorge reported that Japan did not intend to attack the Soviet Union. On 5 December, the Kalinin, Western and Northwestern fronts attacked. Solnechnogorsk was retaken on 12 December, Klin on 15 December, and Tula was liberated on 16 December.",
        "On 8 December, Hitler issued Directive 39, a defensive one. On 20 December, he cancelled the planned withdrawals and ordered every inch of ground defended. Brauchitsch was dismissed on 19 December, and Hitler took personal command of the army. Guderian, Hoepner and Strauss were also removed by 25 December. The Soviet offensive stopped on 7 January 1942, after pushing the Germans back 100 to 250 km."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Estimates of German casualties in the offensive add up to 174,194 killed, wounded or missing, but Soviet and German sources differ. On the Soviet side, one estimate gives about 581,000 casualties, while the figures by phase, published in other sources, add up to more than a million, and the difference comes from different ways of counting.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first time the German army was stopped and pushed back on a large scale, and it ended the idea of a quick victory over the Soviet Union. The reasons for the German failure, according to historians, were: starting late, because of the detour to Kiev, the mud, supply stretched to the limit, the lack of winter clothing and equipment and the underestimation of Soviet reserves. Hitler's decisions in December also counted.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The story of 'Panfilov's 28 men', who supposedly held up a column of tanks near Moscow, was one of the great myths of the Soviet war. An investigation by the military prosecutor, in 1948, concluded that the story was an invention of journalists at the newspaper Red Star. The report stayed secret and was only published in 2015." },
        { note: "The temperatures are debated. Bock spoke of −45 °C, the Soviet records show −28.8 °C in December." },
        { note: "The importance of the battle of Mtsensk, on 6 October, where T-34s ambushed Panzer IVs, is debated. The historian Zetterling notes that the Germans lost only nine tanks." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  leningrado: {
    war: "Eastern Front of the Second World War: the siege of Leningrad (1941 to 1944)",
    hook: "For 872 days, a city of more than three million people withstood a siege designed to starve it to death, and never surrendered.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Leningrad, today Saint Petersburg, had been the capital of Russia and was the cradle of the Revolution. It was home to the Soviet Baltic fleet and produced, in 1939, 11% of the country's industry. For Hitler, it had a symbolic value, and he did not want the city alive. In a directive of 29 September 1941, the German command wrote that, after the defeat of the Soviet Union, 'there can be no interest in the continued existence of this large urban center'. The plan was to raze the city and hand the land north of the Neva river to Finland.",
        "Army Group North, under Field Marshal Wilhelm von Leeb, had 26 divisions, and Leeb thought he would need 35. On 21 September 1941, the German command ruled out occupying the city, because then it would have to feed the population. They chose siege and hunger.",
        { h: "The road to the siege" },
        { timeline: [
          ["August 1941", "The Finns come to about 20 km from the northern suburbs. On 31 August, Marshal Mannerheim halts the offensive, at the 1939 border."],
          ["30 August", "The last railway is cut."],
          ["8 September", "The Germans reach Lake Ladoga, at Shlisselburg. The siege begins."],
          ["7 October", "Hitler reaffirms: no surrender is to be accepted."],
          ["December 1941", "A Soviet counterattack drives the Germans out of Tikhvin, on 9 December."],
          ["18 January 1943", "An offensive opens a narrow corridor by land."],
          ["27 January 1944", "The siege is broken for good."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Wilhelm von Leeb, Georg von Küchler and the Germans" },
        "Leeb commanded Army Group North, and Küchler, the 18th Army. Their task was not to invade the city, but to encircle it and wait. There was no plan to take the city street by street.",
        { h: "Carl Gustaf Mannerheim and Finland" },
        "Finland fought alongside Germany, to retake territories lost in the war of 1939 to 1940. Mannerheim stopped the advance at the old border on 31 August 1941, and the Finnish command refused German requests to attack Leningrad from the air. The Finns reached the Svir river on 7 September and went no farther south. The Finnish role remains debated.",
        { h: "Zhukov, Govorov and Zhdanov" },
        "Georgy Zhukov was the one who organized the defense in the decisive weeks of September. Leonid Govorov later commanded the Leningrad front and directed the city's artillery. Andrei Zhdanov was the head of the Party in the city. Among the Soviet commanders were also Voroshilov, Popov, Meretskov and Admiral Tributs, of the Baltic fleet.",
        { h: "The residents" },
        "The prewar population was about 3.2 million. More than a million civilians were mobilized to dig defenses, including 700 km of antitank ditches. The guns of the cruiser Aurora were taken off the ship and brought to the city's defense."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The initial force was about 725,000 men on the Axis side and 930,000 on the Soviet side, by the most cited counts. The casualty numbers, 579,985 for the Axis and 3,473,066 for the Soviets, cover the whole campaign in the region, not just the city.",
        { h: "The hunger" },
        "The first winter was the worst. The bread [[ration|ração]] fell to 125 grams per day between November 1941 and February 1942, and the bread was 50% to 60% sawdust and other additives. Deaths reached about 100,000 a month in January and February 1942, almost all from hunger.",
        "The political police recorded the first case of human flesh used as food on 13 December 1941. By December 1942, 2,105 people had been arrested for cannibalism, and 1,216 murders over ration cards were recorded in the first half of 1942 alone. Historians point out that cannibalism was rare compared with the scale of the famine.",
        { h: "The Road of Life" },
        "The only open route was Lake Ladoga. In summer, boats. In winter, trucks over the ice, the so-called Road of Life, to Osinovets, and from there by a 45 km suburban railway. It brought food in and took people out, under bombs and artillery fire. On 19 September 1941, an air raid with 276 bombers killed about 1,000 civilians. In total, bombing and artillery fire killed 5,723 and wounded 20,507, according to the same count.",
        { h: "The Seventh Symphony" },
        "Dmitri Shostakovich began the Seventh Symphony in Leningrad and finished it in Kuibyshev, in December 1941. The premiere was there, on 5 March 1942. On 9 August 1942, the Leningrad Radio Orchestra, conducted by Karl Eliasberg, played the work in the besieged city. Only about 15 musicians were left, and they went to look for the others in cold, dark apartments. Musicians received extra rations. The score was flown in, in July.",
        "Loudspeakers broadcast the concert throughout the city and toward the German lines. Beforehand, General Govorov ordered an artillery bombardment of the German positions to silence them, Operation Squall, with about three thousand shells. According to one version, the date was chosen because Hitler had planned a banquet at the Astoria hotel to celebrate the fall of the city on that day."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The city never surrendered. The siege was broken for good on 27 January 1944, after 872 days. The death toll is debated. One of the counts cites 642,000 civilian deaths during the siege, plus 400,000 during the evacuations, and total estimates of 1.3 million (Salisbury) to 1.5 million, and of 1.6 to 2 million (Glantz). About 1.4 million to 1.7 million were evacuated, depending on the source.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The siege is one of the longest and deadliest in history. Hitler had chosen starvation on purpose, and some historians classify it as genocide for that reason. The resistance kept part of the German army tied down for almost three years and became a central symbol of the memory of the war in Russia.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The death toll ranges from 600,000 to more than a million and a half, and the difference comes from how it is counted: deaths in the siege, in the evacuation, civilians only, the city only." },
        { note: "The origin of the Seventh Symphony is disputed. The official version says Shostakovich wrote it in response to the invasion. The violinist Rostislav Dubinsky said the first movement was ready a year earlier, and the book Testimony, by Solomon Volkov, claims that the 'invasion' theme already had other targets. The book's reliability is debated." },
        { note: "The Blue Division, made up of Spanish volunteers, appears in the German order of battle in the region." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  tobruk: {
    war: "Western Desert Campaign, in North Africa (1941)",
    hook: "In a besieged port in the desert, Australian soldiers withstood Rommel's tanks and adopted as a medal the insult that German propaganda gave them.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the end of 1940, the British and their allies had driven the Italians out of much of Libya. Operation Compass, between December 1940 and February 1941, pushed back the Italian 10th Army. Tobruk, a port on the coast of Cyrenaica, was taken from the Italians in January 1941.",
        "After that, many British troops were sent to Greece and Syria. A skeleton force was left in Cyrenaica, with little equipment and transport. The British commander Wavell thought the Germans could not attack before May. Germany, however, sent troops to help the Italians (Operation Sunflower, February to May 1941), under Erwin Rommel, and Wavell proved mistaken.",
        { h: "The road to Tobruk" },
        { timeline: [
          ["January 1941", "The Allies take Tobruk from the Italians."],
          ["February to May 1941", "Rommel's German troops arrive (Operation Sunflower)."],
          ["10 to 11 April", "Axis troops surround Tobruk. The siege begins."],
          ["13 and 14 April", "First major German attack, with tanks."],
          ["June 1941", "Operation Battleaxe, a relief attempt, fails."],
          ["August to October", "The Australian troops are replaced by others."],
          ["18 to 27 November", "Operation Crusader opens the way to Tobruk."]
        ]},
        "Rommel could not go on to Egypt without the port of Tobruk: without it, Axis supply stayed tied to Tripoli and Benghazi, very far away. Leaving an Allied garrison behind his line was too risky."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Leslie Morshead, the commander" },
        "An Australian general who commanded the 9th Division and the entire garrison. He ordered that any enemy penetration be eliminated at once, and the defense gained a second line behind the first.",
        { h: "Erwin Rommel" },
        "Commander of the Afrika Korps, with the 5th Light Division and the 15th Panzer Division, plus the Italian divisions Ariete, Brescia and Trento. The Italian general Gariboldi, Italian commander in the theater, arrived on 28 April. Axis supply ran through Tripoli, Benghazi and Tobruk and suffered from shortages and from ship losses.",
        { h: "Wavell and Auchinleck" },
        "Archibald Wavell commanded the Middle East. After the failure of Operation Battleaxe, in June, he was replaced by Claude Auchinleck."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "In April, there were about 36,000 people in Tobruk, but about a third were base units, refugees or prisoners. Another count speaks of about 35,000 Allies, 14,000 of them Australians. The garrison had the Australian 9th Division, the 18th Brigade, four British artillery regiments and the Indian 3rd Motor Brigade. The antiaircraft defense, the 4th Brigade, had 16 heavy guns and 59 light ones, most of them around the harbor.",
        "The Axis numbers are uncertain and vary depending on the source.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The perimeter, inherited from the Italians, was a double semicircle of concrete strongpoints 13 to 14 km from the port, about 48 km long. It had an antitank ditch on the inside, barbed wire and traps in some stretches. The Allies built a second line, some 3 km behind. Each battalion covered about 8 km of front.",
        { h: "The April attack" },
        "On 13 and 14 April, German infantry opened a passage through the ditches and the wire on the El Adem road, and 36 [[tanks|tanque]] of the 5th Panzer Regiment went through the gap. About 3 km later, they were stopped by [[artillery|artilharia]], by [[antitank guns|canhão antitanque]] and by dug-in Crusader tanks. In one account, the regiment lost 17 tanks in twenty minutes. This number comes from a single secondary source and should be read as an estimate.",
        "On 16 April, at Ras el Medauar, the Australians counterattacked and captured more than 800 prisoners.",
        { h: "The salient" },
        "Between 30 April and 3 May, the Axis occupied a strip about 5 km wide, up to 3 km deep. It was the biggest gain of the siege, paid for with about 650 German and 500 Italian casualties. The garrison reacted and closed the breach.",
        { h: "The siege, May to November" },
        "Operation Brevity (15 and 16 May) tried to break the siege and held almost nothing, despite briefly taking the Halfaya Pass. Operation Battleaxe (15 to 17 June) also failed, with 969 Allied casualties and the loss of many tanks. In July, the 8th Commando attacked the point called Twin Pimples, and took it.",
        "Supply came by sea, in a ferry service that also gave gunfire support, brought fresh troops and took out the wounded. The Navy suffered 780 casualties (539 killed or missing and 241 wounded). 26 warships and other vessels were sunk, plus six merchant ships, and 23 were damaged. One of those sunk was the destroyer Waterhen, hit by Stukas.",
        { h: "The relief of the troops" },
        "General Blamey and the Australian government asked for the withdrawal of the 9th Division, citing the health of the troops and the wish to bring all Australians together under one command. Auchinleck agreed in principle, but delayed, because the relief required fast ships on moonless nights and resources were focused on Operation Crusader. The Australian prime ministers Fadden and then Curtin refused Churchill's requests to reverse it. Poles and Czechs landed from 19 to 29 August, and the British 70th Division arrived in September and October. Command passed from Morshead to Scobie.",
        { h: "The end of the siege" },
        "Operation Crusader began on 18 November. On 21 November, the garrison broke out on the flank and opened a corridor about 13 km wide. On 27 November, it took Ed Duda and linked up with the New Zealanders of the 8th Army. Rommel decided to abandon Tobruk on 5 December, and the siege was lifted for good that same month. The Germans retook the port on 21 June 1942, after the British defeat at Gazala."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Allied ground casualties, from 10 April to 27 November, came to 5,989, including 3,194 Australians (744 killed, 1,974 wounded, 476 missing). Those of the Axis, from 15 February to 18 November, included 1,130 Italians killed, 4,255 wounded and 3,851 missing, and about 538 Germans killed, 1,657 wounded and 681 missing. Axis aircraft shot down range between 74 and 150, depending on the source.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It is usually said to be the first time in the war that [[panzer|panzer]] units were stopped. It showed that the German style of mobile warfare could be contained by a well-organized defense, near the sea, supplied by sea. It tied down at Tobruk troops and supplies that Rommel wanted to use in the offensive toward Egypt.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name 'Rats of Tobruk' is attributed to William Joyce, 'Lord Haw-Haw', who in German broadcasts described the soldiers as living like rats in underground shelters. The Australians adopted the name with pride. Corporal Leslie Dufton made an unofficial medal with the image of a rat, using metal from a German bomber shot down by the garrison with captured weapons." },
        { note: "How long did the siege last? Between 10 April and 27 November it is 231 days. The figure of 241 days, repeated elsewhere, only works if the end is counted in December, around 10 December, the date on which the siege is given as lifted in some sources." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

